rd the Democrats. The Republican incumbent president is term-limited, so both parties will nominate new candidates, and Vermont has backed the Democratic nominee in every presidential election since 1992. No public polls of the race or expert ratings exist yet, and neither side has reported fundraising, so the forecast rests on the state's underlying partisanship.\n\nCurrent headlines concern the 2028 Democratic primary, including early New Hampshire polling and speculation about Bernie Sanders' political succession, rather than a competitive general election in Vermont. For Republicans to carry the state, the national environment would need to swing by historic proportions. The realistic range of outcomes runs from a solid Democratic win to a statewide landslide.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.967, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9556, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9811, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9288, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9351, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9368, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9347, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9639, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9597, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.9666, "margin": 18.0}]}